 Here are key C++ low-latency tricks and tips:

  Compile-Time Optimization

  1. constexpr - Move computations to compile time (as we demonstrated with Fibonacci)
  2. consteval (C++20) - Force compile-time evaluation
  3. Template metaprogramming - Type computations at compile time
  4. if constexpr - Eliminate dead code branches at compile time

  Static Polymorphism

  5. CRTP - Avoid vtable lookups (as we demonstrated)
  6. std::variant + std::visit - Type-safe alternatives to virtual functions
  7. Template specialization - Compile-time dispatch instead of runtime

  Memory Layout & Cache Optimization

  8. Cache line alignment - Avoid false sharing (as we demonstrated)
  9. Structure packing - Minimize padding, keep hot data together
  10. Data-oriented design - Arrays of structs vs struct of arrays (SoA)
  11. Prefetching - __builtin_prefetch() for predictable access patterns
  12. Huge pages - Reduce TLB misses for large data structures

  Memory Management

  13. Object pools - Pre-allocate objects, avoid runtime allocation
  14. Stack allocation - Use std::array instead of std::vector when size is known
  15. Custom allocators - Arena allocators, monotonic allocators
  16. Avoid new/delete in hot paths - All allocation should be done during initialization

  Compiler Hints & Intrinsics

  17. [[likely]]/[[unlikely]] - Branch prediction hints
  18. __builtin_expect() - Help compiler optimize branches
  19. inline/always_inline - Control inlining decisions
  20. restrict keyword - Tell compiler pointers don't alias

  Loop Optimization

  21. Loop unrolling - Reduce branch overhead (as we demonstrated)
  22. Loop fusion - Combine multiple loops over same data
  23. Minimize loop-carried dependencies - Enable better pipelining

  Atomic & Lock-Free

  24. Lock-free data structures - Avoid mutex overhead
  25. std::atomic with memory_order_relaxed - When ordering not critical
  26. Spinlocks - For very short critical sections
  27. Read-Copy-Update (RCU) - For read-heavy workloads

  System-Level

  28. CPU pinning - pthread_setaffinity_np() to avoid migration
  29. Isolate CPUs - Use isolcpus kernel parameter
  30. Disable frequency scaling - Lock CPU to max frequency
  31. NUMA awareness - Allocate memory on correct node
  32. Real-time scheduling - SCHED_FIFO or SCHED_DEADLINE

  Code Structure

  33. Avoid exceptions in hot paths - Use error codes or std::expected
  34. Minimize virtual calls - Know when polymorphism is needed
  35. Avoid std::function overhead - Use templates when possible
  36. Small string optimization (SSO) - Be aware of string allocation thresholds

  Measurement & Profiling

  37. Google Benchmark - Microbenchmark everything (as we're doing)
  38. perf stat - Measure cache misses, branch mispredictions
  39. Intel VTune / AMD uProf - Deep profiling
  40. TSC (Time Stamp Counter) - High-resolution timing: __rdtsc()

  Advanced Techniques

  41. Branch-free code - Use arithmetic/logical ops instead of if/else
  42. SIMD intrinsics - SSE/AVX for parallel operations
  43. Bit manipulation tricks - Fast operations using bit tricks
  44. Lookup tables - Trade memory for computation time
  45. Hot/cold splitting - Separate frequently used code from rare paths

  Common Pitfalls to Avoid

  46. Don't use std::endl - Use '\n' (endl flushes)
  47. Avoid std::shared_ptr in hot paths - Atomic ref counting overhead
  48. Watch out for hidden allocations - Lambdas capturing large objects
  49. Beware of copy elision failures - Use move semantics
  50. Profile before optimizing - Measure, don't guess
